  <title>lua variables</title>
  
<text xml:space="preserve"><note-content version="0.1"><underline>lua variables</underline>

Absolutely! And that rule is actually the correct way to do it. 👍

hl.window_rule({
    match = { title = "^Rename .*" },
    size = "500 50",
    float = true,
    center = true,
})

The reason it works comes from regular expressions (regex). Hyprland uses regexes for many matchers, and they're incredibly powerful once you know the basics.

The ^

^ means:

"the beginning of the string."

So

^Rename

means

Match only if the title starts with Rename.

Examples:

Rename ".bashrc"          ✔
Rename "notes.txt"        ✔
Rename Multiple Files     ✔
Please Rename             ✘
File Rename               ✘

Without the ^

Rename

it means

Find the word "Rename" anywhere in the title.

So these would also match:

Please Rename This        ✔
File Rename               ✔
Window: Rename            ✔
The .*

The dot (.) means

any character

The star (*) means

zero or more of the previous thing

Together

.*

means

anything after this.

So

^Rename .*

reads almost like English:

Starts with "Rename "
then anything.

Examples:

Rename ".dbus"            ✔
Rename "music"            ✔
Rename "myfile.txt"       ✔
Rename                    ✔ (because * means zero or more)
Why not
title = "Rename Multiple Files"

Because that's an exact string.

It matches only

Rename Multiple Files

and nothing else.

That's why it worked for the bulk rename dialog.

The single-file rename dialog changes every time:

Rename ".bashrc"
Rename "notes.txt"
Rename "kernel.config"
Rename "vacation.jpg"

A regex lets one rule match all of those.

The regexes you'll use 90% of the time
Starts with
^Firefox

Matches

Firefox
Firefox - Downloads
Firefox — ChatGPT
Ends with
txt$

The $ means

end of the string.

Matches

notes.txt

Doesn't match

notes.txt.backup
Exact match
^kitty$

Both symbols together mean

start here...
...and end here.

So it matches only

kitty

not

kitty terminal
kitty-1
mykitty
Contains
Steam

Matches

Steam
Steam Library
Launching Steam
One I think you'll love

Suppose one day you want every terminal to float except one.

You can do

match = {
    class = "^kitty$",
}

or

match = {
    title = "^ncmpcpp$",
}

or

match = {
    class = "^mpv$",
    title = "YouTube",
}

You can combine them very precisely.

Since we're building your setup...

I have a recommendation that will save you a lot of headaches later.

Instead of one rules.lua, split it into categories:

~/.config/hypr/

hyprland.lua
look.lua
bindings.lua
input.lua
monitors.lua
autostart.lua

rules/
    dialogs.lua
    terminals.lua
    media.lua
    games.lua
    utilities.lua

Then your hyprland.lua simply requires them:

require("rules.dialogs")
require("rules.terminals")
require("rules.media")
require("rules.games")
require("rules.utilities")





I want to use the program awww.
I load the daemon as a systemd service, and then I need to give it an order via "awww img". issue is "awww img" does not recognize folders, only 1 file at a time.
i dont want a script running in the background with a folder variable and a sleep command, I want a systemd timer that executes "awww img" every X time and randomizes the file each time, something similar to what this script do with the input folder:





Method 1: Set in hyprland.confYou can configure Hyprland to launch the application using X11 every time you run it. Add this to your hyprland.conf:ini# GTK apps
env = GDK_BACKEND,x11

# Qt apps
env = QT_QPA_PLATFORM,xcb

# SDL apps
env = SDL_VIDEODRIVER,x11
Use code with caution.Note: This sets the environment variable globally, which applies to all launched applications.Method 2: Launch via the TerminalIf you only want to force an X11 backend for an app on a one-off basis or in a script, run the specific command in the terminal:For GTK Apps (e.g., Thunar, GNOME Text Editor):GDK_BACKEND=x11 app-nameFor Qt Apps (e.g., VLC, qBittorrent):QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb app-nameMethod 3: Edit the Application .desktop FileTo ensure an app always opens on X11 when launched from your application launcher (like Rofi or Wofi), you can override its .desktop file:Copy the .desktop file to your user directory:cp /usr/share/applications/appname.desktop ~/.local/share/applications/Open the file in a text editor.Find the line that starts with Exec= and modify it to include the environment variable.For example: Exec=env GDK_BACKEND=x11 appname %UIf you tell me what specific application you are trying to run, I can give you the exact environment variable or startup flag required for that specific program.








